            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 32,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I'm all for general purpose optimizations, but currently the peak memory usage is firmware update, which uses the full image size in ram (2X that actually), so the savings of 400k doesn't really change anything at a product level.  Is there a reason this is where you started?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 34,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Did you look at all at optimizing the router itself?  This isn't something I'm aware anyone has even looked at.\n\nKeeping all the routes in the router has some advantages from a security perspective, in that we know at startup exactly what routes exist and which permissions each of them has.  Your patch opens some doors for possible permissions issues (first patch had a pre-auth CVE).\n\nIf you could look into doing your optimization on the code, and not modify the routes, I'd appreciate it."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Gunnar Mills",
                        "email": "gunnar@gmills.xyz",
                        "username": "gtmills"
                    },
                    "message": "Unaddressed comments"
                },
